title: 'Temporal Equivalence Principle: Native hi_class Conformal Implementation, Linear Perturbation Closure, and CMB Acoustic Peak Preservation'
abstract: |
  Standard cosmology explains the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) acoustic peaks, the pre-recombination sound horizon, and the thermal scaling relevant to Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN) within an FLRW expansion history conventionally extrapolated toward a physical singularity. This paper demonstrates that the CMB acoustic-sector and conformal thermal/sound-horizon scalings are preserved with high fidelity under a static conformal temporal-transport geometry governed by the Temporal Equivalence Principle (TEP).
  In the TEP framework, matter clocks and photon phases evolve in a causal matter metric defined by a conformal scalar field g̃_μν = A(φ)² g_μν. Because this conformal transport geometry is mathematically isomorphic to the FLRW scale factor a(t), standard Boltzmann solvers like hi_class and CLASS can be used as conformal-frame calculators for the background/acoustic-sector mapping tested here. The parameter traditionally identified as Dark Energy (Ω_Λ) is operationally reinterpreted within this implementation as the homogeneous temporal-shear background contribution filling the same background budget slot, Ω_φ.
  This paper implements the native TEP interpretation directly in hi_class. Within the broader TEP interpretation, by recognizing that the spatial metric does not stretch, the "Big Bang" is reinterpreted not as a physical density singularity, but as a TEP temporal horizon—an asymptotic boundary where the observational clock map A_clock → 0. Direct Boltzmann integration verifies this background/acoustic mathematical isomorphism, verifying the internal conformal-frame preservation of the acoustic sector — the pre-recombination sound-horizon ratio is rₛ^TEP/rₛ^Λ CDM=0.999994 (corresponding to a <6 ppm deviation) and the acoustic-peak morphology remains intact without invoking early-universe spatial expansion. It is stated plainly that this preservation is a mathematical identity by construction — identifying A(φ) with a(t) reproduces the FLRW integrals by definition, so the <6 ppm residual is a code-level artefact of finite-precision reconstruction and the exercise verifies the hi_class conformal-frame implementation, not the TEP hypothesis itself. The acoustic indistinguishability is nonetheless the necessary consistency requirement a conformal-frame alternative must satisfy: a framework that failed it would be excluded, and the demonstration that early-universe background physics cannot distinguish a stretching spatial metric from an evolving conformal clock-rate field shifts the discriminating burden to the perturbation and late-time sectors where the models do differ.
  Beyond the background mapping, the paper closes the linear pure-conformal scalar perturbation sector through the EFT closure α_M=-2α_A, α_B=2α_A, α_K=w_c α_A², α_T=0, where w_c = Z_E - 6β_A² is the effective cosmological kineticity parameterized by the Einstein-frame kinetic coefficient Z_E = P_,X + 2XP_,XX of the scalar (with the exact nonlinear principal-operator matching treated separately in Appendix A.5a). Evolving δφ natively through the full Einstein–Boltzmann hierarchy — the regular δφ variable being required because x_smg = δφ/φ' is singular at the α_A = 0 turnover — reveals a structure invisible to the no-ghost discriminant D = α_K + (3)/(2)α_B² = (w_c+6)α_A²: the canonical Einstein-frame image Z_E = 1 (α_K = -5α_A², the closure quoted in earlier versions of this work) passes D > 0 yet is linearly unstable on the cosmological background, carrying a tachyonic growing mode that produces runaway structure growth. The sign-corrected unscreened branch (w_c = +7, i.e. Z_E = 13) is stable but mediates an O(1) scalar-tensor fifth force, yielding σ_8 ≃ 1.6 — observationally excluded. The viable branch is the kinetically screened regime furnished by the nonlinear P(X) sector: for Z_E ≳ 7×10² the scalar response decouples and growth recovers the ΛCDM amplitude (σ_8 = 0.824 versus 0.823; median matter-power deviation ≲ 0.2%, peaking at ∼ 1.2% at the low-k end, with a residual ∼ 13% low-ℓ ISW signature), while Z_E ∼ 6×10³ gives σ_8 ≃ 0.76, coincidentally at the weak-lensing central value. The measured σ_8(Z_E) response curve is the perturbation sector's central result; the closure and its screening parameter Z_E are falsifiable in both amplitude and scale dependence. An earlier statement that the active scalar contributes bitwise-identically to the background-only spectra is retracted: that result was produced while the SMG perturbation sector was silently disabled by a background-density gate, and it measured only the background channel.
  A joint hi_class Cobaya MCMC tests the finite-turnover TEP conformal background within the native hi_class implementation. Two production runs are reported. The primary inference uses Planck 2018 low-ℓ TT/EE + lensing + BAO + Pantheon+, in which the TEP homogeneous amplitude is not detected: a converged four-chain MPI suite (4,814 post-burn-in samples, max parameter R-1 = 0.011) gives ε_T = 0.0062 ± 0.0046 (median 0.0053, ε_T < 0.015 at 95% CL), consistent with zero at ∼ 1.3σ — a null result that bounds rather than confirms the conformal modification. The full-likelihood extension adds planck_2018_highl_plik.TTTEEE_lite to the same baseline: a converged four-chain MPI run (11,237 post-burn-in samples; max parameter R-1 = 0.0455) yields ε_T = 0.0043 ± 0.0025, a one-sided bound ε_T < 0.0087 at 95% CL at fixed turnover scale z_T=5 (ε_T < 0.0103 at 95% CL under z_T marginalization, Section 4.5), against a matched two-chain ΛCDM reference (max R-1 = 0.0069). On identical likelihoods the models are statistically indistinguishable: Δχ² = -0.11 at the respective best fits, and a matched-parameter evaluation verifies the ε_T → 0 limit reproduces ΛCDM to Δχ² = 0.007. The bound is geometric rather than dynamical: at fixed cosmology the high-ℓ likelihood contributes Δχ² ≃ +69 at ε_T = 0.008 through the anchored acoustic scale θ_s, while low-ℓ TT and Pantheon+ weakly prefer positive amplitudes (Δχ² ≃ -0.6 each). An earlier clipy overflow report is resolved: the warnings persist in the completed run but are benign, the historical failure tracing to a stock-classy build shadowing the patched hi_class on the Cobaya theory path. Companion TEP-C0 (Paper 26) reports Pantheon+ model-comparison evidence for the physical no-Λ temporal-shear branch: Bayes factor approximately 5.8 for the conservative zₗₒₛ=5 branch (substantial, not decisive), approximately 5.4 for the fixed zₗₒₛ=100 benchmark, and approximately 5.4 for the broad free-zₗₒₛ branch. These late-time model-comparison results are imported, not re-derived in HC; HC supplies the native hi_class acoustic and perturbation closure. Within the broader TEP corpus, Paper 11 interprets the Hubble tension as a late-time, environment-dependent clock-transport effect caused by the environmental screening of the scalar field, rather than through a crisis in early-universe physics; that interpretation is likewise imported — HC does not independently re-analyse the distance-ladder data, and Paper 11 Step 62 derives the Cepheid response scale |κ_P²^pred(λ_0=1)| = 1.196× 10⁶ mag from the stellar-envelope density ratio ρ_T/⟨ρ⟩(10 d) = 8.45× 10⁵ at the unit-transport benchmark λ_0 = 1 with bare coupling |β_A| = 1 fixed by Paper 0 (matching the SH0ES fit (1.191± 0.480)× 10⁶ mag to 0.5%).
  The Planck lensing likelihood in the baseline hi_class implementation retains a standard pressureless CDM component (Ω_cdmh²=0.1133±0.0061 in the active posterior; 0.11813±0.00117 in the full high-ℓ TTTEEE active-perturbation posterior) because isotropic conformal rescaling g̃_μν=A²(φ)g_μν stretches spatial rulers alongside clocks and cancels out of null rays, leaving only 8.72% of C₁₀₀^φφ when omega_cdm is removed (χ²=1265.73 on the 9 Planck 2018 CMBMarged lensing bandpowers; Step 21). Under the canonical unified matter metric g̃_μν=-A_loc²(u_grav(x))U_μ U_ν+Aₛₚ²(u_drift(t,x))Π_μν+B(φ)Π_μ^αΠ_ν^β∇_αφ∇_βφ (U_μ≡-∇_μφ̄/√(2X̄)) — where the (3+1) ADM spacetime lapse rescales as N_TEP(x)=A_loc(u_grav(x))N_GR(x) while at a fixed cosmic epoch (Aₛₚ(t₀)=1) the spatial 3-metric γ̃_ij=γ_ij remains unstretched, so intermediate clock rates A_loc(u_grav(x)) refract photons along the line of sight — the selected two-branch kinetic sector P_,X=k√ξ+2ξ (k=16.03 fixed by a₀, zero per-system free parameters) amplifies baryon-only cosmic-web and cluster-outskirt lensing potentials by M=3.07–6.18 (1×) to 5.15–11.37 (2×), boosting C_ℓ^φφ by 9.4×–38.3× (1×) to 26.5×–129.3× (2×). Fitting the full multipole spectrum (ℓ∈[2,2500]) across the Silk transition directly against the official 9-bin Planck 2018 CMBMarged lensing likelihood at ω_cdm=0 yields χ²=15.40 with fixed Silk geometry (ℓ_Silk=300,α_Silk=2) and χ²=13.40 (χ²/N=1.49, Δχ²=+1.11 relative to the 12.30 CDM baseline and Δχ²=-1252.33 relative to bare no-CDM; Step 21), reproducing the observed Planck CMB lensing spectrum across L∈[8,400] without particulate dark matter. Furthermore, because the proper-time field φ fills all space as a continuous ambient scalar medium (A(φ)>0 everywhere) with fixed action scale gₜ=Λ_X²/(√(2)M_Pl)=cH₀/2 and conformally invariant sound-horizon acceleration ratio qₛ=g_b(rₛ)/gₜ=Ω_b(H₀ rₛ/c), the same zero-parameter two-branch clock-slope equation ∇·[P_,X(|∇ u|)∇ u]=β_Aρ_b evaluated at the sound horizon (qₛ=1.62×10⁻³, yₛ=7.38, ω_φ=0.1166 in the single-pass rₛ=146.19 Mpc analytic integral; converging under self-consistent rₛ(ω_φ) fixed-point iteration in hi_class to rₛ=144.94–145.00 Mpc, qₛ=1.59–1.60×10⁻³, yₛ=7.42–7.44) predicts a pre-boundary baryon clock-slope well-driving strength ω_φ=ω_b(yₛ/√(2))=0.1179–0.1182 (ω_m,eff=0.1404–0.1406; spanning ω_φ∈[0.1174,0.1199] across H₀∈[67.4,70.0] km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹, all within ≲1.6σ of the unconstrained full-Planck TTTEEE MCMC posterior 0.11813±0.00117 and within 0.02σ [0.02%] at the posterior mean H₀=68.18 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹). Evaluated in exact hi_class on linear cosmological scales (k≲0.2 h Mpc⁻¹, R≳10 Mpc, where 99.8% of the R₈=8 h⁻¹Mpc σ_8² variance originates at k≤1.0 h Mpc⁻¹, so applying the nonlinear halo-interior transition filter above k_*=1.5 h Mpc⁻¹ shifts σ_8 by only 1.0% from 0.8185 to 0.8103, whereas inside virialized galaxy halos R∈[1,100] kpc for M_b=6×10¹⁰ M_⊙, the algebraic Gauss law (k/√(2))q y²+q² y³=1 smoothly transitions M_eff(R)=1+y(R)/√(2) from 6.26 on linear scales through 5.18 at 100 kpc, 2.25 at 30 kpc, and 1.42 at 10 kpc down to 1.12 at 3 kpc and 1.04 at 1 kpc without an independent NFW particle cusp), this zero-parameter baryon clock-slope response reproduces the acoustic scale (100θ_s^CLASS=1.04201, +0.10σ from the TTTEEE posterior 1.04198±0.00031 at H₀=68.18 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹, and 100θ_s=1.04111, +0.02σ from 1.04110±0.00031 at H₀=67.95 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹), the first three acoustic peaks to ≤0.31%–0.40%, the linear-scale matter power spectrum P(k) with σ_8=0.8170–0.8186 (S₈=0.8221–0.8226), the BAO drag scale r_d=147.49–147.55 Mpc, the SDSS DR12 consensus BAO + RSD likelihood (χ²/N=0.68–0.78 on 6 BAO points, 0.71–0.76 on 9 BAO+fσ_8 points), and the full Planck 2018 TTTEEE + low-ℓ + lensing + BAO + Pantheon+ likelihood with Δχ²=-0.06 (ΔAIC=-2.06 with one fewer free parameter relative to free ω_cdm; Step 21 Part C).
  The imported C0 evidence is prior- and anchor-sensitive: the conservative Bayes factor falls to approximately 0.63 after removing z < 0.023 anchors. The high-turnover branches are mathematical shape benchmarks pending an environment-dependent physical realization.
